#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "9662033b-2a3a-5d80-91ad-ed542898b80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14544" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841,$ find $\\mu.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.8159\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.8159 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ \\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8159)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8159) = 0.90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & =0.90 \\\\ 4-\\mu  & =5.4 \\\\ \\mu  & =-1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "35cc4c4c-79fb-5ae2-94e0-79f007af832b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14545" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.4013 \\\\  & =0.5987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.5987 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5987)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.5987) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.25 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "2a8633b4-f937-57e3-899d-c3176f07a48f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14546" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 <        X <        3.18) = 0.4038,$ find $\\sigma.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.18<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ P(0<X<3.18) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-P(X<0) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-0.5 & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.18-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ \\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7019) = 0.53.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & =0.53 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/id #uuid "5eb435ff-ef12-5f18-89d6-4ac163fae344" :question/math-academy-id "e-14548" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P( -4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu) = 0.6528,$ find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$*" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu <     X <     \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <       6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -5\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6\\mu -\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ \\frac{5\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8264)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8264) = 0.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\mu }{2} & =0.94 \\\\ 5\\mu  & =1.88 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1515f028-5354-5d83-8e17-a9ccf1040c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366079} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7d0d5f-ca2d-54e5-97a2-dc467638d2e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366080} {:answer/id #uuid "2826ba70-6812-5c13-a1a6-ef7c8a15574d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366081} {:answer/id #uuid "bef0f8c0-81c5-5280-9c4c-fe16fbe8e694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366082} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5473b1-6f07-5b40-93ad-06108620444a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef0f8c0-81c5-5280-9c4c-fe16fbe8e694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366082} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7cf02d-a6cc-593c-82e5-aa24c4fd5436" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a25085a-365b-5ddc-b16d-221db979b7e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132145" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ with $P(X\\ge -1)=0.5793,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.4960\\,$ | $\\,0.4920\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =P(X<-1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge -1) \\\\  & =1-0.5793 \\\\  & =0.4207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =0.4207 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.2)=0.4207\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)=-0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & =-0.2 \\\\ -1-\\mu  & =-1 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c0a4f83-bbb1-550c-bd30-59dd7d0a4c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366086} {:answer/id #uuid "5425cc37-bba1-51de-9c5f-b9ee06e973a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366087} {:answer/id #uuid "47876aed-7aef-5d05-83ad-fa9d6649f5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366088} {:answer/id #uuid "d215c933-0286-5e03-a59a-849da8aa1104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366089} {:answer/id #uuid "d88a8c87-8aaa-58bd-ada7-2181b447c324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47876aed-7aef-5d05-83ad-fa9d6649f5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366088} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a40dcc0-6e8a-5378-8423-45352037f1bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "342b789c-c374-5492-aa7a-28c22db880ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-132147" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{2}^{2})$ with $P(X>2)=0.3085,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =1-P(X>2) \\\\  & =1-0.3085 \\\\  & =0.6915\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.6915 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ \\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.5)=0.6915\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)=0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & =0.5 \\\\ 2-\\mu  & =1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550612b0-f868-5593-bf17-1f4801eced5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366093} {:answer/id #uuid "3b44c125-eeef-5b98-aff7-89f0c42e8342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366094} {:answer/id #uuid "e8972dcb-5a72-5e87-9c4d-80e9367b4b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366095} {:answer/id #uuid "52d69119-02e2-5898-a594-f8d1a84b5354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366096} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3af835-9bda-5a04-9fbf-a5984651cae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3af835-9bda-5a04-9fbf-a5984651cae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366097} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c760c72-f840-50b2-b869-fb250b7a4334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a8d2514-0a41-5c99-8d02-4165002366d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132312" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-18<X<10)=0.1113,$ find  $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ | $\\,0.5120\\,$ | $\\,0.5160\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-18<X<10$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =-4.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-18<X<10)=P(-4-14<X<-4+14)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ 2P(-4<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ P(-4<X<10) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-P(X<-4) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-0.5 & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{10-(-4)}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{14}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ \\frac{14}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)=0.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{\\sigma } & =0.14 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{14}{0.14} \\\\ \\sigma  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111026b5-18d3-55ca-8bb5-cee3498381ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366100} {:answer/id #uuid "f01e101d-c6b9-5c9a-bc3e-f33aabb05c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366101} {:answer/id #uuid "d69b07ce-bcc3-5ece-8187-01a6fb580b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366102} {:answer/id #uuid "97f79d87-c55c-5b0c-a3cd-745c22f17ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366103} {:answer/id #uuid "7d98e4bb-a486-56ca-aada-2c54fa95bf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01e101d-c6b9-5c9a-bc3e-f33aabb05c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d8c6a53-8410-5556-89fe-79d1a808c8fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fe23eed-7aab-5669-902a-c1f67966047a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132316" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-5\\mu <X<7\\mu )=0.5762,$ determine $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu )=0.5762,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-0.5 & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7\\mu -\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{6\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881) \\\\ \\frac{2\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.8)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\mu }{5} & =0.8 \\\\ 2\\mu  & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a05f50-996c-56f7-b190-5b6b70f571c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366107} {:answer/id #uuid "1f276d13-9c52-5b24-ab9a-7c7e39eb0d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366108} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfe58d0-d99a-5c55-ba41-05d320580b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366109} {:answer/id #uuid "602a2b64-2325-5506-9688-e5f58aef5760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366110} {:answer/id #uuid "bff6dd16-f902-5d11-a57d-44af403e77ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff6dd16-f902-5d11-a57d-44af403e77ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366111} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9040198-9d4c-5e4c-8ad4-cbae76be0e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f7271d0-e47b-5c15-88d7-62cc3a1c70f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-210662" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2.7<X<2.7)=0.4108,$ find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.7<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ P(0<X<2.7) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-P(X<0) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-0.5 & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.7-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7054 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma }) & =0.7054 \\\\ \\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.54)=0.7054\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)=0.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & =0.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2.7}{0.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465ecb4c-2258-508d-a8c3-0f020da44388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465ecb4c-2258-508d-a8c3-0f020da44388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366114} :answer-field/id #uuid "0666d02b-c3ff-55d1-91c7-bc06f4fd5539" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58648273-ca39-594e-9ffd-cfe23f0e9128" :question/math-academy-id "q-276190" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2})$ with $P(X<5)=0.7734,$ find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 5)=0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5-\\mu }{6}) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5-\\mu }{6}) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{5-\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-\\mu }{6} & =0.75 \\\\ 5-\\mu  & =4.5 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa73f493-33cb-59af-aa6a-b8cead222dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366117} {:answer/id #uuid "39cf6617-d5e2-5082-b7cd-b60115897998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39cf6617-d5e2-5082-b7cd-b60115897998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366118} :answer-field/id #uuid "362fbe42-a99b-5b9e-a8a5-d10221d4f6a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90d3ebb6-0267-588e-b32d-6f8b780ca99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-276213" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3<X<3)=0.8472,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<X<3) & =0.8472 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3) & =0.8472 \\\\ P(0<X<3) & =0.4236 \\\\ P(X<3)-P(X<0) & =0.4236 \\\\ P(X<3)-0.5 & =0.4236 \\\\ P(X<3) & =0.9236 \\\\ P(X\\le 3) & =0.9236\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.9236 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.9236 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.9236 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9236)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.43)=0.9236\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9236)=1.43.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.43 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.43} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe7e0662-a078-54b7-a813-ad74b0ec9d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366121} {:answer/id #uuid "ca180f76-8ee5-5f66-ab70-17ae4f76df77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe7e0662-a078-54b7-a813-ad74b0ec9d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366121} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f6f7fa-09b1-51e9-86d2-b034b85c81dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c891ce1-94fb-5e63-a985-2a4cdc995d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-276229" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<3)=0.6046,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<3)=P(-3<X<3).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<X<3) & =0.6046 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3) & =0.6046 \\\\ P(0<X<3) & =0.3023 \\\\ P(X<3)-P(X<0) & =0.3023 \\\\ P(X<3)-0.5 & =0.3023 \\\\ P(X<3) & =0.8023 \\\\ P(X\\le 3) & =0.8023\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.8023 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8023 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.8023 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8023)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.85)=0.8023\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8023)=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =0.85 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{0.85} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4eddea4-a928-5897-b3f0-fedd5b7e5d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366125} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0295cd-34cc-5be8-890b-0e759a031058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4eddea4-a928-5897-b3f0-fedd5b7e5d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366125} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d3296b-5063-5b1b-8068-847bbaf30c18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ee54add-b18e-5650-af34-a4cdc6b7d93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-276251" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-1<X<7)=0.8948,$ use the table to find  $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | $\\,0.9484\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-1<X<7$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =3.$*\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-1<X<7)=P(3-4<X<3+4)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<7) & =0.8948 \\\\ 2P(3<X<7) & =0.8948 \\\\ P(3<X<7) & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7)-P(X<3) & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7)-0.5 & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7) & =0.9474 \\\\ P(X\\le 7) & =0.9474\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =0.9474 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.9474 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.9474 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9474)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.62)=0.9474\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9474)=1.62.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.62 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{1.62} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.47.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a455784-6319-5e26-a4a9-7f0a4ed965ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a455784-6319-5e26-a4a9-7f0a4ed965ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366129} :answer-field/id #uuid "b400011a-03d7-59d3-9dbc-7c8d2c51171d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80493d5e-8752-55d2-9110-3cf07a3e8430" :question/math-academy-id "q-276252" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{7}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3\\mu <X<5\\mu )=0.6476,$ use the table to determine $\\mu ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-3\\mu <X<5\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -4\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu )=0.6476,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-3\\mu <X<5\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -4\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.6476 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.6476 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(X\\le 5\\mu ) & =0.8238\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5\\mu -\\mu }{7}) & =0.8238 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4\\mu }{7}) & =0.8238 \\\\ \\frac{4\\mu }{7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8238)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.93)=0.8238\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8238)=0.93.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\mu }{7} & =0.93 \\\\ 4\\mu  & =6.51 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{6.51}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & ≈1.63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c1f29e-c495-5bdb-ba82-fc4f56b050ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366132} {:answer/id #uuid "770fa1df-b6dc-5d05-8236-61c7749e9e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "770fa1df-b6dc-5d05-8236-61c7749e9e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366133} :answer-field/id #uuid "0922c097-e22d-5539-9023-4d5bb76a14d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb0df213-45cc-53dd-bead-cd278fd56e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-339311" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Suppose that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5.5}^{2})$ and $P(X\\le 18.2)=0.5871.$ Find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 18.2) & =0.5871 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.22)=0.5871\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)=0.22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & =0.22 \\\\ 18.2-\\mu  & =1.21 \\\\ \\mu  & =16.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7c6c21c-481a-5065-8c24-6fc0f30e8326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366136} {:answer/id #uuid "d80ac706-0332-5061-b5b1-41c50a660959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80ac706-0332-5061-b5b1-41c50a660959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366137} :answer-field/id #uuid "42616fee-8a37-5d1c-9afa-a3289c81bdd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a588f01c-265d-5d84-975d-fded865f91df" :question/math-academy-id "q-341492" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1.5,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<2)=0.5910,$ use the table value to find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 2)=0.5910\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.5910 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5910 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5910 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.23)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)=0.23.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.23 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.23} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.173\\,91,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(2.173\\,91)^{2}≈4.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521e3e5c-5fc8-5648-b3d6-a222d101a861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521e3e5c-5fc8-5648-b3d6-a222d101a861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366140} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd8aead-8fdd-5a26-8845-6018cd4ada7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "086757bf-08b6-53b0-8588-8b61e7c9dc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-341651" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X>1)=0.0618,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ | $\\,0.9394\\,$ | $\\,0.9406\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9495\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =1-P(X>1) \\\\  & =1-0.0618 \\\\  & =0.9382\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.9382 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.54)=0.9382\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)=1.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "688e0c79-a1ba-541c-ba58-3b68604598f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366143} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed6e6d-bc00-5ba7-b722-300fe8bf4b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688e0c79-a1ba-541c-ba58-3b68604598f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366143} :answer-field/id #uuid "718b3acd-fe69-57b7-b872-9b9e8f9b347e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaf97213-6022-5ef3-82bd-5721b5b3d67d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341655" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 1)=0.8413,$ use the table to find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453939c1-28f8-5f3b-be36-7b0414f0db31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453939c1-28f8-5f3b-be36-7b0414f0db31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec906fff-67bf-59be-8a0a-9cf7628c2dfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "446faabc-91f1-514d-a381-8d4843d76498" :question/math-academy-id "q-341991" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2<X<2)=0.6578,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.95)=0.8289\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.95 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.95} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.105\\,26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.105\\,26)^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc0693-2b95-5d68-9efa-4c06083e5258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34cc0693-2b95-5d68-9efa-4c06083e5258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150} :answer-field/id #uuid "a309ef5f-2dca-5d76-9389-bfe4594aeaa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a514ca2-c609-5f32-878a-96963ce73dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342070" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(2<X<6)=0.2812,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $2<X<6$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =4.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1809fa-2d90-5e50-8d6d-d8145cbe4784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366153} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc0a61e-4733-56ee-827a-d6678420805f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366154} {:answer/id #uuid "57663cda-a4a9-5405-8ff2-311ec1e58a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366155} {:answer/id #uuid "c213ce73-a590-57dd-a9fa-e64adb77c139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366156} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc4e2a1-61b2-5421-b990-b24875f38cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc4e2a1-61b2-5421-b990-b24875f38cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366157} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8d8767-fcc4-5c8b-be82-c5f8e7fac386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c28e986-ea7b-50ed-a863-862a49696b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-94828" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{7}^{2})$ with $P(X<6)=0.6736,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6)=0.6736\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 6)=0.6736\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6736 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6-\\mu }{7}) & =0.6736 \\\\ \\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.45)=0.6736\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)=0.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-\\mu }{7} & =0.45 \\\\ 6-\\mu  & =3.15 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41b2f5c4-1373-5bad-8b2f-719684d7d4ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366160} {:answer/id #uuid "f60c3f3a-4350-532c-9991-cb7330fb03a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366161} {:answer/id #uuid "b0768511-9828-5ef1-89fb-029273cdaeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366162} {:answer/id #uuid "cff984b6-bc18-516e-a116-1cb84eab1607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366163} {:answer/id #uuid "8569960d-3c69-58e9-be4d-74f05046fdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41b2f5c4-1373-5bad-8b2f-719684d7d4ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366160} :answer-field/id #uuid "031df20c-c7fb-5b8b-807b-0051efa0d353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f23ee09-5084-5ca1-a1ad-f14a4dc56458" :question/math-academy-id "q-94856" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-3.5,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-2)=0.7257,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-2)=0.7257\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -2)=0.7257\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -2) & =0.7257 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-2+3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.7257 \\\\ \\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7257)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.60)=0.7257\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7257)=0.60.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & =0.60 \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2.5}^{2}=6.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ebc809-6a4e-5bdb-b0f6-6e9717a9be17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366167} {:answer/id #uuid "8c02e807-1b9f-5629-8f41-ce39e721d371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366168} {:answer/id #uuid "bf19ad81-1f08-5569-8091-db3eb69f35db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366169} {:answer/id #uuid "58eb3375-ce46-5a5f-9c05-3135650375af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366170} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc06f4-45d3-5f3a-93f4-752ca13ba54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60cc06f4-45d3-5f3a-93f4-752ca13ba54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366171} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5911f7-319f-527a-becd-4210f8f3cb25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "996e50f0-5ba7-5009-aee8-4f22fc33cb83" :question/math-academy-id "q-94857" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1.8,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 4)=0.2119,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X\\ge 4) \\\\  & =1-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-1.8}{\\sigma }) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{2.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.80)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.80.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.2}{\\sigma } & =0.80 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2.2}{0.80} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2.75}^{2}=7.56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab90957b-206e-541c-b3e0-40deacd9bc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110196} {:answer/id #uuid "b18d11f8-2b80-50a8-98b2-4d634e744f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110197} {:answer/id #uuid "c35148cc-b8e6-5ac7-aac6-e81e8071c142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110198} {:answer/id #uuid "20c2b68e-e133-5da1-a51f-514b1f9985b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110199} {:answer/id #uuid "59d47685-43c2-55b1-a86a-71905719c776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18d11f8-2b80-50a8-98b2-4d634e744f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110197} :answer-field/id #uuid "15701758-eeb3-5aac-812f-2fe665fc4e8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0de7ef2c-c461-55a3-92ec-92284bc49b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-94892" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<9)=0.5468,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<9)=P(-9<X<9).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-9<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<9) & =0.2374 \\\\ P(X<9)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{9}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{9}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(12)^{2}=144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
